    "type": "question",
    "label": "84",
    "prompt": "Use the identity $a^3-b^3=(a-b)(a^2+ab+b^2)$ to rationalise the denominator of\n$$\\frac1{\\sqrt2-\\sqrt[3]2}.$$",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\dfrac{(2+\\sqrt2\\sqrt[3]2+\\sqrt[3]4)(\\sqrt2+1)}2$.\n\nUse the cubic identity, then rationalise $2(\\sqrt2-1)$.",
      "worked": "The supplied identity uses $a=\\sqrt2$ and $b=\\sqrt[3]2$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac1{\\sqrt2-\\sqrt[3]2}&=\\frac{2+\\sqrt2\\sqrt[3]2+\\sqrt[3]4}{(\\sqrt2)^3-(\\sqrt[3]2)^3}\\\\\\frac1{\\sqrt2-\\sqrt[3]2}&=\\frac{2+\\sqrt2\\sqrt[3]2+\\sqrt[3]4}{2(\\sqrt2-1)}\\\\\\frac1{\\sqrt2-\\sqrt[3]2}&=\\frac{(2+\\sqrt2\\sqrt[3]2+\\sqrt[3]4)(\\sqrt2+1)}{2(\\sqrt2-1)(\\sqrt2+1)}\\\\\\frac1{\\sqrt2-\\sqrt[3]2}&=\\frac{(2+\\sqrt2\\sqrt[3]2+\\sqrt[3]4)(\\sqrt2+1)}2.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
